pecifically with  his grandmother and then when it cut to  him smiling demonically but on top of  that just knowing the pain and suffering  that this kid must be feeling through  that it was heartbreaking as well as any  portion of flashback featuring Shinra  and his mother playing hero because  society here seemed so set up to  immediately classify him as the villain  I mean you've got the smile not to  mention the shop shop thief's and on top  of that he can even conjure flames which  seems to be seen as the great enemy of  the world so and it's not a wonderful  start to life if your goal is to become  a hero  of course shinra's tragedy runs deeper  than that with the death of his mother  and brother for which he seems to be  blamed for it along with that we also  had this stunning shot of an incredibly  ominous looking fire bro which was great  because it really gave me those  exaggerated Soul Eater vibes which was  an aesthetic I adored for that series  but the author looks to have toned down  and  for fire force but all the lore  intriguingly this is also accompanied by  silhouette image of Shinra throwing out  the question of did he or didn't he  potentially even implying that he has  some kind of grand force within him  outside of his control whatever the case  is definitely something special about  the kid which makes sense he's a  journeyman character and all as for the  rest of the main cast they're a bit more  difficult to judge at this point in time  because it was a very Shinra heavy  episode as it should have been at the  moment my favorite is probably Aki taru  because he strikes me as a strong leader  despite the fact that he doesn't seem to  have any special pyrokinetic abilities  there was also Tucker heesu who has a  serious stick up his ass for which I  imagine we're going to discover the  reasoning behind and he's like super  super intense size and overall he's a  pretty good balance to a Qatar is more  laid-back nature I will say that I'm not  entirely convinced by his weapon of  choice being the Uzi like gun and that's  because despite the horrific power in  reality that is very rarely conveyed  through animation or even film and more  often than not just sounds like it never  really has any effect plus it just  doesn't look cool to hold I mean you've  got this super amazing in serious  character with a bulky bulky frame due  to the coach holding this teeny-tiny gun  thing there you know isn't going to do  any serious damage to what if the big  that is  so if taka hiza is the like firearms  dude and I'm hoping that he has other  weapons of choice and this was just a  bad first outing with us but if I  remember correctly he's also a  pyrokinetic yeah so I don't know it just  seems like a bit of a waste to have him  wielding guns but then again what do I  know I'm just some schmuck who's only  seen the first episode feel free to  change my mind enemy rounding out the  team we also have two female members who  of course were more or less introduced  in the traditional female way with a  communal shower scene at the moment I'm  more drawn to marki because she exudes  that sense of fun and also she's  absurdly shiny I mean just look at all  those light bounces she must be  permanently coated in oil however I'm  also intrigued by iris if only for her a  specific role on the team which I  believe is existing to facilitate the  spiritual passing of defeated infernals  or something along those lines question  mark I'm not really sure because maybe I  just missed it during this episode but  if she does have any spiritual abilities  I don't see them just shots of her  praying so at the moment she very much  represents religion in the real world  needing to take a leap of blind faith  rather than being shown in any sort of  proof I mostly find her presence  interesting though because it  potentially places a strict condition  under which the infernals can be dealt  with like will the team refuse to take  out an infernal without iris air to  offer a prayer for the soul in question  or what happens if there are two  infernals and iris can  to deal with one there's some  interesting moral questions that have  the potential to develop as a result of  the focus on the salvaging of souls and  it seems religion in general certainly  can't be ignored in fire force but with  their entire facility being in a  Cathedral and all and this is a side  note I've only just seen this but I  really enjoy how the five full station  is just sort of graffiti'd onto the side  of the cathedral which brings something  else up I'm curious as to exactly why  these guys have been thrown into such a  place  the infernal seem to be an incredible  threat to society at large one that only  a very specialized group seem capable of  dealing with so you'd figure that they  would possibly get some more decent  funding for their base of operations  yeah I'm sure that's something that will  be explained in time like maybe there's  a societal prejudice against what they  do or if selecting that Cathedral was a  choice made by the team itself for its  spiritual significance by the way it's  mildly intriguing as is the world in  general I do quite like the aesthetic  presented in fire forces a lot of care  and detail that has gone into making it  feel like a well lived in world which I  appreciate and I cannot wait to learn  more about the history of it as well as  the infernals and speaking of I've  brought them up many many many times  already but I haven't commented on just  how fantastic these guys look in both  cases during this episode I felt it was  a bit of a shame when the infernals were  defeated because I just loved looking at  them so much the primarily black figure  shrouded in flames as a treat for the  old eyeballs and actually just the fire  in general was handled so damn well by  this first episode and you Tobit would  be given that fire is in the title of  the series and the basis of seemingly  everything but it was nailed and full  credit to David Productions for stepping  up to the plate and crafting something  that beautiful speaking of David  productions were a company I was quite  unfamiliar with up until watching this  first episode and upon looking into them  they haven't really done a whole lot  outside of the Jojo adaptations which I  haven't seen but from here on out I will  be keeping a close eye on this company  so in the end fire force did pretty much  everything I would have wanted to series  opener to do it gave us a compelling  protagonist a solid setting an extended  cast for the nice chunk of potential and  brilliantly animated fired e-mini things  so as a result I'm in hit me with  everything you've got  but with that said I'm still not sure if  I'll be doing weekly reviews on it so  let me know if that's something that you  would like to see but that pretty much  does it for my first impressions of fire  force if you enjoyed this video and the  content this channel produced in general  then please do consider donating to the  new world review patreon because the  support of all of your amazing people is  what continues to make this channel  possible and if patreon isn't quite your  style then please do leave this video a  like share or subscribe because it also  but this channel an incredible amount  and if you'd like to join the fun at any  time then please do head over to my  discord server where a wide array of  shenanigan retakes place on a daily  basis and finally please do comment with  your thoughts on fire force this has  been the new world review and I'll see  you next time

